  "entry": "E28",
  "article": "A Utah microreactor just powered an Nvidia AI chip live on stage, a first for next-generation nuclear in America, and the two companies used the moment to announce a 30-megawatt AI factory in the desert designed to drink zero local water",
  "gold_statement": "Governor Spencer Cox responded with an executive order putting guardrails on data center development",
  "gold_meaning": {
   "who": "Utah governor",
   "what": "executive order setting guardrails on data center development",
   "status": "happened"
  },
  "draws": {
   "A1": [
    "More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .",
    "So the event a nuclear startup and Nvidia staged in the Utah desert on July 1 was aimed squarely at that mood. Valar Atomics wired a Blackwell -based Nvidia desktop unit to its Ward 250 microreactor, served a website off the electricity, and announced a plan for something much bigger that skips the local water entirely.",
    "Alongside the demo, the two companies announced an agreement to jointly explore nuclear-powered AI systems. The concrete version under study is a 30-megawatt closed-loop AI factory in Utah, which Valar’s own announcement says “consumes no water from the local community.”",
    "“Through this work with Valar Atomics, Nvidia is exploring how behind-the-meter, waterless advanced nuclear systems could support future AI factories,” John Josephakis, a global vice president at Nvidia, told Reuters.",
    "Utah spent the spring arguing over Kevin O’Leary’s Stratos data center project in Box Elder County, with public pushback centered on the Great Salt Lake, and Governor Spencer Cox responded with an executive order putting guardrails on data center development."
   ],
