  "entry": "E38",
  "article": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "gold_statement": "Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way",
  "gold_meaning": {
   "who": "analyst Richard Windsor",
   "what": "NVIDIA's China position is about zero and will stay so; DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China; the development will not affect NVIDIA",
   "how_much": "about zero",
   "status": "forecast"
  },
  "draws": {
   "A1": [
    "According to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.",
    "Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.",
    "DeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months. The hiring process has been conducted discreetly, without publicly posting job openings on general recruitment platforms.",
    "\"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\" said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.",
    "Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.",
    "However, that dominance is increasingly being challenged as several local technology companies, including Alibaba and Baidu, develop their own AI chips.",
    "In April, DeepSeek launched its V4 model, which had been optimised for Huawei Ascend chips, while Huawei revealed that training for the V4-Flash model also used Ascend processors.",
    "The launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.",
    "OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom",
    "Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.",
    "Reuters previously reported that the company was preparing its first funding round worth US$7 billion, with a valuation of between US$52 billion and US$59 billion, ending a years-long strategy of rejecting outside investment."
